{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"415e92c138204f92d99edc1efd28e2b1558cf171c27b0ac5b54e2c637dbd117c","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_95bbcfda78ae05dadc92c67ddf31cfe440889fc4f0c52e6b083f81181e9f26f9","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a coherent and technically sound framework for model-based reinforcement learning in stochastic dynamical systems. The methodological integration of Bayesian Neural Networks with stochastic inputs and alpha-divergence minimization is well-motivated and mathematically consistent. Experimental validation is robust, utilizing a challenging benchmark (Wet-Chicken) and industrial datasets, with appropriate statistical reporting. The work is complete, with clear algorithmic descriptions and reproducible details.","paper_id":"H1fl8S9ee","paper_identity_sha256":"b6458b653b59e340d47c0e0a5417624f6db27a2888bd78d964ac4d3cfdc714f5","prompt_identity_sha256":"a15a4e80a505592c81526e0c77d47857d4652e72a57b789ce7a2724a123ae5bd","raw_response_sha256":"a93563c06ec2aa85116bafcdbcad67c0f5c19964aca9175d435c837415b32993","request_identity_sha256":"4c3c25c3e9ec075617cc5da787b786927b96a060995c8b057ce9337f7540a12b","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"ede3a6569dad33afb5a799fe8188057d517db5713b1a14fbb7ad9528f8ffdb66","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-007","SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-020","SRC-021","SRC-022","SRC-023","SRC-024","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The proposed method is technically sound and well-formulated. The authors extend standard Bayesian Neural Networks (BNNs) by introducing a stochastic input variable z to capture complex transition dynamics, such as multi-modality and heteroskedasticity, which are typically missed by Gaussian assumptions. The mathematical derivation is consistent, clearly defining the likelihood, priors, and the intractable posterior. The use of alpha-divergence minimization (specifically alpha = 0.5) is well-justified as a balance between mode-seeking and mass-covering properties, with the energy function (Equation 8) providing a scalable optimization target. The policy search algorithm (Algorithm 1) correctly employs stochastic gradient descent with the reparameterization trick to optimize the policy parameters by minimizing the expected cost over Monte Carlo roll-outs. The distinction between parameter uncertainty (integrated over W) and environmental stochasticity (integrated over z) is clearly articulated and correctly implemented in the predictive distribution (Equation 14). No conceptual or implementation errors are apparent in the described methodology.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-028","SRC-029","SRC-030","SRC-031","SRC-032","SRC-033","SRC-034","SRC-035","SRC-036","SRC-037","SRC-038","SRC-039","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The experimental design is comprehensive and effectively validates the claims. The authors evaluate the method on three distinct scenarios: the Wet-Chicken benchmark, a gas turbine application, and an industrial benchmark. The Wet-Chicken problem is particularly well-chosen as it features bi-modal and heteroskedastic dynamics, directly testing the core capability of the proposed stochastic BNNs. The inclusion of a Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO-P) baseline provides a strong upper-bound reference for policy performance. The industrial applications demonstrate practical relevance, with the gas turbine experiment specifically designed to test robustness to missing features by using a world model with reduced input dimensions. The evaluation metrics are appropriate, including Mean Squared Error (MSE) and test log-likelihood for model accuracy, and average reward for policy performance. The results consistently show that the proposed method (alpha = 0.5) outperforms or matches the performance of Variational Bayes (VB) and Gaussian Processes (GPs), particularly in capturing complex stochastic patterns. The ablation-like comparison between different alpha values (0.5, 1.0) and VB provides insight into the sensitivity of the divergence measure.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-016","SRC-020","SRC-025","SRC-027","SRC-038"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for reproducibility. The algorithm (Algorithm 1) is clearly specified, including the sampling procedure for weights and noise variables. Key hyperparameters are discussed, such as the prior variances lambda and gamma, with the specific choice of gamma = d (data dimensionality) justified to maintain the effect of the stochastic input. The optimization procedure using stochastic gradient descent and the reparameterization trick is described. The experimental setup details, such as the number of trajectories (100) and their length (1000) for the industrial benchmark, and the train/test split (70/30), are provided. The use of Theano for automatic differentiation is mentioned. While specific code availability is not explicitly stated in the provided text, the level of detail in the mathematical formulation and algorithmic steps is high, enabling a skilled practitioner to replicate the core methodology. The description of the gas turbine data preprocessing and the world model construction is clear enough to understand the experimental design.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-017","SRC-026","SRC-028","SRC-032","SRC-034","SRC-040"],"review_text":"The paper positions itself well against prior work. It correctly identifies the limitations of existing model-based reinforcement learning approaches, such as Gaussian Processes (GPs) and standard neural networks, in capturing complex stochastic dynamics like multi-modality and heteroskedasticity. The comparison with Variational Bayes (VB) is particularly relevant, as VB is a standard technique for BNNs, and the paper demonstrates the superiority of alpha-divergence minimization in this context. The Wet-Chicken benchmark is a well-known challenge in the field, and the authors claim to provide the first model-based solution, which is a significant contribution. The industrial benchmark is cited from recent literature (Hein et al., 2016b), ensuring the relevance of the comparison. The related work section adequately covers policy gradient methods and model-based approaches, highlighting the novelty of using BNNs with stochastic inputs for policy search. The comparisons are fair, using the same data and evaluation metrics across all methods.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-026","SRC-027","SRC-030","SRC-031","SRC-033"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor is appropriate for the type of experiments conducted. The authors report average values over 5 runs with respective standard errors for both model test error (MSE, log-likelihood) and policy performance (average reward). This provides a measure of the variance across different random initializations or data splits, which is crucial for assessing the reliability of the results. The use of standard errors allows for a basic assessment of the significance of the differences between methods. The average rank over all runs is also provided, offering a robust summary of performance across the different benchmarks. While formal hypothesis tests (e.g., t-tests) are not explicitly reported, the consistent outperformance of the proposed method across multiple runs and benchmarks, along with the reported standard errors, provides sufficient evidence to support the claims. The variance in the VB method is noted to be larger, which is an honest and useful observation regarding the robustness of the different approaches.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"245f0ab69fa311ad243d48739b06b74491acab2ff02f77004564577cc608aaf3","paper_id":"H1fl8S9ee","paper_ordinal":91,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"a93563c06ec2aa85116bafcdbcad67c0f5c19964aca9175d435c837415b32993","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"H1fl8S9ee","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"5f92f57ae17e17cbffdb2e93926530c2bab14f6d2e4f5bff5d00869158701d55","request_identity_sha256":"4c3c25c3e9ec075617cc5da787b786927b96a060995c8b057ce9337f7540a12b","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1823,"prompt_tokens":20858,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":22681},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"4c3c25c3e9ec075617cc5da787b786927b96a060995c8b057ce9337f7540a12b","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}